# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# driftcode environment
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values as each chapter needs them.
# `.env` is gitignored - never commit real keys.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Chapter 1: server ---------------------------------------------------
PORT=4000
DRIFT_API_URL=http://localhost:4000

# --- Chapter 4: database -------------------------------------------------
# Postgres connection string. Neon (neon.tech) has a free tier.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require"

# --- Chapter 5: the models -----------------------------------------------
# At least one of these is required. The server reports which models it can
# run at GET /models, and the picker greys out the rest with the reason.

# Anthropic key from console.anthropic.com -> API keys.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."

# OpenAI key. Also add a model entry to packages/shared/src/models.ts with
# provider: "openai" to make it selectable.
# OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-..."

# --- Chapter 10: auth (optional) -----------------------------------------
# Leave these unset and the server runs single-user: no sign-in, one implicit
# owner for every session. Set them and people sign in with a browser and each
# get their own sessions.
#
# Clerk (clerk.com, free tier): Configure > Developers > OAuth applications.
#   - Scopes: openid, email, profile
#   - Turn on "Public" (the CLI cannot hold a secret, so it uses PKCE)
#   - Redirect URI: http://localhost:4000/auth/callback
#
# CLERK_FRONTEND_API="https://your-app.clerk.accounts.dev"
# CLERK_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=""
# CLERK_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=""
#
# Signs the token the server hands the CLI after sign-in. Any long random
# string: openssl rand -hex 32
# JWT_SECRET=""
#
# Must exactly match the redirect URI registered above.
# PUBLIC_URL="http://localhost:4000"

# --- Chapter 11: billing (optional) --------------------------------------
# Leave unset and nothing is metered: unlimited use, no balance shown, no
# /upgrade command. Set them and each turn is charged against a credit balance.
#
# A credit is one cent of model spend, computed from the rates in the model
# registry - so an Opus turn costs visibly more than a Haiku one.
#
# Polar (polar.sh). Create a meter with exactly these settings:
#   Name:                 driftcode_credits
#   Filter:               name equals driftcode_usage
#   Aggregation:          Sum over the metadata property "credits"
# Then a meter-credits benefit attached to a one-time purchase product.
#
# POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=""
# POLAR_CREDITS_METER_ID=""
# POLAR_PRODUCT_ID=""
# POLAR_SERVER=sandbox
